  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1030 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe warm and mostly dry pattern is likely to continue through at\nleast the first half of this week, with the upper ridge persisting\nbut becoming flattened as a wave ejects onto the northern Plains.\nDespite this, upper level heights actually rise across the \nsouthern US by midweek, with latest guidance suggesting 500mb \nheights of 596-598 dam. In response to that northern US wave, \npressure falls in the lee of the Rockies will induce modest \nsoutherly flow locally Mon-Wed (sustained 10-15 mph winds). This \nsfc flow pattern, combined with the gradually increasing upper \nheights, makes me wonder if the NBM is a few degrees too cool on \nthe high temps (it has had a cool bias in similar setups this \nsummer). The NBM forecast has highs in the mid 90s on the Caprock \nand near 100F off the Caprock, so regardless, temperatures will \nremain above average through mid-week. \n\nThat midweek waving passing to the north could push a front south\nacross the central Plains, but most guidance stops it north of our\nforecast area Wed-Thurs. However, late in the week and into next \nweekend, the upper ridge shifts towards the SW CONUS, introducing \nNW aloft which could open the door to occasional precip chances.\n\nIt has been a lengthy stretch of summer heat. KLBB and KCDS are\nsitting at 50 and 51 consecutive days above 90F, respectively, and\nseveral more such days are on the way. Both of these streaks rank\namong the top 10 longest for those airports (although it pales in\ncomparison to the 100-day streak of 90F+ days that occurred in \n2011). At last, there are at least some hints of relief from the \nheat in the extended forecast. While the deterministic model blend\nkeeps temps warm (in the mid 90s/low 100s) into next weekend, \nindividual NBM members depict a 10-40% chance of highs below 90F. \nThis is by no means a guaranteed pattern change, but at this \npoint we'll take any hints of relief that we can get.",
